## Extinction law info file.
## This specifies certain properties about the extinction law that are used during SED fitting and saving of results

## The short-hand name of the law, that will be stored in the results table
name  Chapman2009Dense

## Construction: the KMH94 R_V=5.5 curve (whitney.r550) with its 1-30 micron opacity column replaced by a log-log-linear interpolation through ten nodes -- the curve's own opacity at 0.9 and 30 um, and eight points at J,H,Ks,[3.6],[4.5],[5.8],[8.0],[24] (this survey's own band wavelengths, definitions.BANDS) scaled to the curve's own Ks opacity; V and the ultraviolet are the unmodified curve.
## [3.6],[4.5],[5.8],[8.0],[24] A_lambda/A_Ks: Chapman et al. 2009, ApJ 690, 496, Table 3 ("Average Relative Extinction A_lambda/A_Ks"), "Our data" row "1<A_Ks<=2": 0.60+-0.05, 0.46+-0.05, 0.44+-0.05, 0.43+-0.05, 0.61+-0.08 at 3.6,4.5,5.8,8,24 um, read from https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/0809.1106 and used verbatim (owner ruling). The table's other high-column row, "A_Ks>=2" (0.64+-0.03, 0.53+-0.03, 0.46+-0.03, 0.45+-0.03, 0.34+-0.13), is not used: the ramp hands this law to every source above A_K=1 with no upper bound (LAW_RAMP_HI, no further bin), and fewer than a tenth of any region's sources sit above A_K=1 and almost none above 2, so the ramp's own regime is measured by the "1<A_Ks<=2" row, not an average with the rarer, higher-column "A_Ks>=2" one.
## J,H A_lambda/A_Ks: Chapman et al. 2009 Table 3 carries no near-infrared columns. Read instead off this curve's own (unmodified KMH94 R_V=5.5) opacity at J (1.235 um) and H (1.662 um) -- 2.453707 and 1.532926 -- within 0.05 of Indebetouw et al. 2005's measured 2.50/1.55 (ApJ 619, 931, Table 1), the near-infrared law being the same host curve in all three papers.

## The column indices of the wavelength and opacity columns in the sibling .par file
colidx_wav   0
colidx_extinction  3

## The ratio of AV / AK in this law
AV_over_AK  7.050377
